---
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { theorems, deferred } from '../../data/formalization';
import { papers, citedElsewhere } from '../../data/papers';
import { TESTS, numberWord } from '../../data/evidence';
import { LEAN_URL, THEOREM_MAP_URL, LEAN_STATUS_URL, PAPERS_URL, CRATE_URL } from '../../consts';

const title = 'Proof | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'What Lean proves, what the tests check, which claim Lean refutes, which targets have no Lean proof, and which papers the crate implements. Each row names the file that shows it.';

const cap = (s: string) => s.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
const split = (s: string) => {
  const [file, item] = s.split(' :: ');
  return { file, item };
};

const cited = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length > 0);
const used = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length === 0 && (p.usedBy ?? []).length > 0);
const uncited = papers.filter((p) => p.citedFrom.length === 0 && (p.usedBy ?? []).length === 0);
/** Papers the sensing page cites for the field's state. */
const onSensing = ['2608.25563', '2410.07720'];
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/proof/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Proof</p>
    <h1>{TESTS.proved} theorems proved, {TESTS.count} tests passing, {TESTS.deferred} targets without a proof.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      {cap(numberWord(TESTS.proved))} theorems are proved in Lean, and a Rust test checks each one
      on numbers. The suite runs {TESTS.count} tests, and all of them pass. Lean also proves that
      commuting operators can stop commuting under a partial trace, which refutes the claim that
      a partial trace preserves commutation. {cap(numberWord(TESTS.deferred))} targets have tests
      and no Lean proof.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="theorems">
    <h2>Which theorems are proved?</h2>
    <p>
      The table lists {TESTS.proved} theorems: six laws of the partial trace, the boundary theorem
      and the commutator identity, the two statements of the counterexample below, two linearity
      laws for the Choi action, and two showing that exact abstraction links compose. The rows
      come from the quantum section of
      <a href={THEOREM_MAP_URL}><code>lean/THEOREM_MAP.md</code></a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pinned Mathlib has no partial trace and no Choi–Jamiołkowski layer, so the proofs build
      both from first principles under
      <a href={LEAN_URL}><code>lean/DeepCausalityFormal/Quantum/</code></a>. The Rust witness is the
      test that checks the same statement on numbers.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Statement</th><th>Lean declaration</th><th>Rust witness</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {theorems.map((t) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Statement"><span class="num">{t.statement}</span><br /><code class="tid">{t.id}</code></td>
              <td data-label="Lean declaration"><code class="item">{split(t.lean).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.lean).file}</span></td>
              <td data-label="Rust witness"><code class="item">{split(t.witness).item}</code><br /><span class="fpath">{split(t.witness).file}</span></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="refuted">
    <h2>Which claim turned out false?</h2>
    <p>
      The claim that a partial trace preserves commutation is false. Take operators X and Y on a
      system with parts A and B, with <code>[X, Y] = 0</code>. Lean proves an explicit pair for
      which <code>[Tr_B X, Tr_B Y] = [[0, 4], [−4, 0]]</code>, which equals
      <code>+4i·σy</code>. So two operators that commute on the whole system can have traced parts
      that do not.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two related statements are true. The boundary version (Q-PTP) holds: if the boundary
      operator commutes with M, its kept-side part commutes with <code>Tr_B M</code>. The
      commutator identity (Q-PTT), <code>Tr_B([Z⊗1, M]) = [Z, Tr_B M]</code>, holds with no
      hypothesis.
    </p>
    <p>
      The identity lets the crate turn a measured residual into a bound. A residual ε on
      <code>[Z⊗1, M]</code> bounds <code>[Z, Tr_B M]</code> by <code>√(d_B)·ε</code> in Frobenius
      norm, where <code>d_B</code> is the dimension traced away. A commutator within tolerance
      therefore certifies the traced conclusion at <code>√(d_B)</code> times that tolerance and
      never the exact one, and the check returns the bound with a flag against the caller’s
      tolerance.
    </p>
    <p>
      The boundary theorem assumes exact equality over a commutative ring, which floating-point
      arithmetic cannot supply. The bound covers that gap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="tests">
    <h2>What do the tests cover?</h2>
    <p>
      {TESTS.count} tests pass under
      <code>cargo test -p deep_causality_quantum --all-features</code>: 892 unit and integration
      tests and one doc test. They include cases where a check must reject its input and cases
      where a check has nothing to examine, such as an empty fault set. Every example printed
      identical output when run twice on one machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      A grep for the test attribute returns a different number, since it counts attributes and the
      test runner counts the tests it runs.
    </p>
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/tests`}>The test tree ↗</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="deferred">
    <h2>Which targets have no Lean proof?</h2>
    <p>
      {cap(numberWord(TESTS.deferred))} targets have no Lean proof: the Choi–Jamiołkowski
      reconstruction isomorphism and six theorems about quantum causal models. The theorem map and
      <a href={LEAN_STATUS_URL}><code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code></a> name them, and the crate carries
      numerical or property-test witnesses for each.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code> says these proofs need net-new Mathlib machinery. For
      <code>quantum.unitary_factorization</code> it names the machinery: direct-sum and
      C*-algebra theory that Mathlib lacks.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      {deferred.map((d) => (
        <div>
          <dt><code>{d.id}</code></dt>
          <dd>
            <p class="dstate">{d.statement}</p>
            <p class="dtoday">{d.today}</p>
          </dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p>
      <code>LEAN_QUANTUM.md</code> lists the Markov freeze check, the naturality check, the
      structural precheck, the fault propagator and the decoder validation as witnessed by tests;
      none claims a theorem. The <a href="/checks/">checks page</a> marks each check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="papers">
    <h2>Which papers does the crate implement?</h2>
    <p>
      {cap(numberWord(cited.length))} of the {TESTS.papers} PDFs under
      <a href={PAPERS_URL}><code>papers/</code></a> are cited from a module in
      <code>src/</code>, and the first list gives each with what the crate takes from it. The
      second list holds the {numberWord(used.length)} that the sensing examples and the
      verifications cite. The third holds the {numberWord(uncited.length)} that no code
      cites; a committed PDF alone shows no implementation.
    </p>
    <ol class="papers">
      {cited.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          {p.usedFor && <p class="pused">{p.usedFor}</p>}
          <p class="pcite">Cited from <code>{p.citedFrom.slice(0, 4).join(', ')}</code>{p.citedFrom.length > 4 ? ` and ${p.citedFrom.length - 4} more` : ''}.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3 id="sensing-papers">Cited by the sensing examples and the verification</h3>
    <ol class="papers">
      {used.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          {p.usedFor && <p class="pused">{p.usedFor}</p>}
          <p class="pcite">Cited by <code>{(p.usedBy ?? []).join(', ')}</code>.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3>Committed and cited by no code</h3>
    <ol class="papers">
      {uncited.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="ptitle">{p.title}</p>
          <p class="pmeta">{p.authors}. {p.ref}, {p.year}.</p>
          <p class="pcite">
            No code, test, README or changelog in the crate cites it.{onSensing.some((id) => p.ref.includes(id)) && (
              <> The <a href="/sensing/#why">sensing page</a> cites it for the field’s state.</>
            )}
          </p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>

    <h3>Cited from the source, not committed</h3>
    <ul class="papers">
      {citedElsewhere.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <p class="pmeta">{c.work}</p>
          <p class="pcite">Cited from <code>{c.citedFrom}</code>.</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .tid { color: var(--fg-2); background: none; padding: 0; font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); }
  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .table-scroll td code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .table-scroll table { table-layout: fixed; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(1) { width: 29%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(2) { width: 31%; }
    .table-scroll th:nth-child(3) { width: 40%; }
  }
  .item { background: none; padding: 0; color: var(--fg-0); font-size: var(--t-mono-sm); }
  .fpath { color: var(--fg-2); font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  .dstate { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); color: var(--fg-0); }
  .dtoday { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }

  .papers { list-style: none; margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); padding: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); max-width: var(--measure); }
  .papers li { padding: var(--space-4) 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .papers p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-1); }
  .ptitle { color: var(--fg-0); font-weight: var(--fw-heading); }
  .pmeta { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pused { color: var(--fg-1); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite { color: var(--fg-2); font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
  .pcite code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
